{
  "comment": "Shared contract for the CAD ref grammars: the engine's cases are asserted by two languages, so they cannot drift. An engine document's (.xbf) refs are the engine's grammar -- REF = [FILE '#'] SELECTOR (',' SELECTOR)*, SELECTOR = OWNER | [OWNER '/'] ELEMENT, OWNER = SEGMENT ('/' SEGMENT)*, SEGMENT = LABEL | LABEL '.' N | QUOTED ['.' N], ELEMENT = an element name | face N | edge N | vertex N. `refCases` are parsed by cadRefs.js's parseEngineRef and cadgen.engine.refs.parse: the document (`file`), each selector's owner (its segments, a quoted one as {quoted, index}; null for the document's root) and element (null for the owner itself), and each selector's text (`printed`); a refused ref parses to nothing. `spellingCases` are served structures and the canonical ref of each occurrence (`at`: its child indices from the root) and element, what the CAD Viewer copies: asserted against cadRefs.js's spellEngineRefs and against cadgen.engine.refs.resolve_structure, which `cadgen inspect` runs, each resolving to its occurrence or element and being its canonical ref. A STEP's refs keep their own grammar until the engine's carries over: `selectorCases`, `inheritanceCases` and `tokenCases`, asserted by cadRefs.js. A STEP ref names geometry: an occurrence token (`o:` + 12 hex) and an element name under an occurrence token or a part label, joined by `/`. An element name, in both grammars, is its kind (`f:`/`e:`/`v:`) and its derivation -- an atom (a feature, a label or `function.operationN`, and the element's number among what it made; or a folded derivation: a feature, `~` and 12 hex) and the steps features took (`;M:`, `;G<kind>:`, `;MG<kind>:`, `;U[n]:`, `;L:`, each with a feature and up to four `+<kind>:<atom>`, or `;D<n>`) -- or, for an element no construction names, its geometric token (12 hex). To every parser a name is an opaque string the grammar admits. STEP cases tagged phase 1 cover label selectors.",
